I believe that And is right about the contrast between [ihi] and [ixi] bein=
g
almost nil when being heard.  I recorded my voice to test this.  At first m=
y
[ihi] and [ixi] sounded distinct, but then I realized that I was pronouncin=
g
[ihi] with a breathy voice [h] (i.e. [=C9=A6], often an allophone of /h/ in
English e.g. {behind}).  When I use a true voiceless [h], the contrast with
[x] almost vanishes in the [i_i] position.  However, the [=C9=A6] v. scrape=
less
[x] contrast does work in my ear, though it is probably not the easiest for
everyone.

I'm guessing that [=C9=A6] is already a de_facto allophone of /'/ in Lojban=
 as
spoken by Anglophones.

